Constructor test suite
======================

Nothing here is part of the submission. No file in tests/ appears in
project/order.txt or is touched by project/compile.

Running
-------

    cd project && ./compile order.txt constructor
    cd ../tests && ./run.sh                 # every case
    ./run.sh geese                          # only cases whose name contains it
    CTOR_BIN=/path/to/constructor ./run.sh  # a binary built elsewhere

run.sh prints one PASS or FAIL line per case, the diff for each failure, and a
count at the end. It exits non-zero if anything failed.

How expectations were written
-----------------------------

Every expected file was written by hand from the specification text before the
program was run, and the program was presumed wrong wherever the two
disagreed. Five defects were found this way and are recorded at the end of
project/uml_changes.txt. Where the specification defines no output, the case
uses a check script that asserts only what the specification does require and
prints the current behaviour so it can be reviewed.

Case layout
-----------

Each directory under cases/ is one case:

    cmd        command-line arguments, one line
    in         standard input
    expected   exact expected standard output (optional)
    check      bash assertions, run in the work directory (optional)
    needs      files to copy from data/, one name per line (optional)
    keepboard  compare the board drawings too (optional)
    <other>    copied into the work directory, e.g. a save file for this case

Each case runs in a fresh temporary directory, so files a case writes
(backup.sv, saves) cannot leak into another case.

Two normalisations keep expected files hand-writable. Trailing whitespace is
stripped from every line, so the "> " prompt of 4.5 reads as ">" when it is
the last thing on a line. And the 41-line board drawings are removed unless
the case asks for them, since only board-matches-course-rendering asserts the
drawing itself; a prompt printed immediately before a drawing survives as a
line of its own.

Determinism
-----------

Cases that assert exact output use -seed and a fixed layout, and loaded dice
so the roll is an input rather than a random draw. The paths that are
irreducibly random are asserted as invariants instead: the multiset of
resources and values on a generated board, the reproducibility of fair dice
under a fixed seed, the number of resources lost to the geese, and that a
stolen resource is one the victim held.

data/
-----

    layout.txt              the layout supplied with the course files, which
                            is also the board of Figure 3
    rectangular_layout.txt  the rendering supplied with the course files
    save_file.txt           the save file supplied with the course files
    spec-figure3.layout     the layout printed in section 3.8
    figure3-empty.sv        that layout with four empty builders, used to
                            render the board with nothing built on it
